Unreleased
    AI repairs allow four attempts per release by default and start at least
    ten seconds apart across installer processes. --repair-limit is an
    optional run-wide cap, not a default that stops the batch after one
    release. --repair-interval controls the spacing.
    Stop repair attempts when an agent leaves the source unchanged. Receipts
    mark the unchanged attempt so missing external prerequisites do not spend
    the remaining repair budget on identical retries.
    install --prefix set to the interpreter's own prefix installs into its
    site library, which @INC already searches, instead of PREFIX/lib/perl5.
    --layout auto|site|install_base (or install.layout) chooses explicitly;
    other prefixes keep the INSTALL_BASE layout. Receipts record the layout
    and any older copy under PREFIX/lib/perl5 that would hide the new one.
    Failed releases enter a repair queue while independent builds continue.
    --repair-jobs controls repair workers separately from --jobs; batches
    default to four build workers and one repair worker.
    Builds find C libraries installed by Homebrew: its include, lib and
    lib/pkgconfig directories are added to CPATH, LIBRARY_PATH and
    PKG_CONFIG_PATH. --native-prefix DIR (or install.native_prefixes)
    chooses other prefixes, --no-native-prefix none; receipts record them.
    Releases whose META.yml holds JSON, or whose metadata names the main
    module instead of the distribution, install instead of failing.
    Configure, build, test and install phases time out after --phase-timeout
    seconds (default 3600), stopping every process the phase started.
    The CPAN package index is parsed once per run instead of once per
    lookup, which makes resolving many modules much faster.
    install accepts several modules or --from FILE and keeps going past
    failures, reporting the outcome of each requested module.
    install-ported accepts --jobs K and builds the selected releases with
    the parallel scheduler; progress lines mark resumed entries.
    Repair calls can be capped per run with --repair-limit when requested.
    install accepts --jobs K (or install.jobs in the config) to build
    independent releases in parallel. Receipts record phase start and finish
    times.
    Rebuild from clean sources after an external repair, so make programs
    with one-second timestamps cannot test stale build products.
    Tests compare canonical paths and pass on macOS.
    Receipts record the platform: operating system, architecture name,
    compiler, make program, thread support, and XPerl commit.
    Add install-ported, which rebuilds every release recorded as successful
    in the ports registry with a dry run, filters, resumable state, and
    JSON and text reports.

